Z||000012||JayUSA77||07-04-2001||09:13 PM||||I dont know about you guys but when i put my deck in (pioneer deh5200) i had to rip out that @^@&*%! metal thing that holds the stock deck in place.  I got it out eventually but im just curious how did you guys get that out. I just ripped it out with vicegrips <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>-J<P>------------------<BR>86' Black Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000013||tgowens||07-04-2001||10:03 PM||||Scott,<P>this is the radio that I install in all of my Fieros.......<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Gmplayer_5.jpg">  <P>It works nicely with the factory sub although you have to splice the two pink wires from the sub amp to a yellow wire from the car harness leading into the radio.......you also need the adapter........<P>Roy, Bobby Newell sold his business a couple of months ago so that link probably won't work. <P>Jason, I bend the bracket back far enough to insert the tip of a pry bar under it and then just snap it out......<P>The reason for removing the triangular bracket is this, the older stock radio used a bertically mounted 12 pin plug. The newer models use a horizontally mounted 21 pin plug (hence, the adapter) and if you try to install the radio without removing the triangular bracket in the rear, the bracket will push on the side of the plug and pop the other end out of connection and the radio will lose power.<P>Also, when I install these I use a 1/8" spacer between the radio bracket and the support frame (slide onto the 7mm screws) in order to align the radio flush with the trim bezel when it is re-installed.<P>I hope this helps..........<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero/index.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>                    <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero/3GTS_SIG.gif">                     </A><p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 07-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||mrfixit58||07-05-2001||08:52 AM||||If you clik on the above link it will lead you to the new owners. Their web address is: <A HREF="http://mnrelectronics.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://mnrelectronics.com/</A>  <P>I can only hope that they can live up to the great customer service I received from Bobby Newell.<P>T.G.Owens - that's the exact radio I put into my 87 and I love it!<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||6
Z||000015||Raydar||07-05-2001||09:22 AM||||M&R seem to be good folks. Bought their CD player 16205302 (I think that's correct) and wiring harness adapter for my car. Had it installed in just a few minutes. They're NOT inexpensive, though. <BR>The radio I bought was refurbed. They told me that ahead of time. <BR>But it looks and works like brand new.<BR>I'm very satisfied.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>You may be able to get the newer version for a bit less money. I chose this one because it looks "stock".<P>I would also recommend a pair of Pioneer 4x10s for the dash. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 07-05-2001).]||||reg||14
